/target

# Local secrets for the cloud compose stack. Only .env.example is committed.
#
# Every variant, not just `.env`: `.env.local` and `.env.production` are the
# ones people create without thinking about it, and a filled-in one holds the
# database password and both service secrets.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Leteo's own memories, in Leteo's own repository.
#
# The README tells teams to commit their `.leteo/` — that is how a project
# shares what it has learned, and those chunks belong in version control. This
# repository is the exception rather than a contradiction: the `.leteo/` here
# would be the maintainer's own store, which is a record of somebody's working
# day rather than a fact about the code.
/.leteo

# Databases, wherever they land.
#
# The tests build theirs in the system temp directory, but `--data-dir` and
# `--database` take any path, and a store created while trying something out in
# the checkout is somebody's memories: titles, prompts, and whatever was pasted
# into a session. The sidecars go too — a `-wal` holds recently written rows.
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# Agent scratch state.
#
# Per-machine and per-session: a lock file naming the local scheduler, and
# OpenCode's job queue keyed by a session id. Neither says anything about the
# project, and both churn on every run.
#
# `.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock` was covered only by `.git/info/exclude`, which
# belongs to one checkout and travels with nobody — so it was ignored here and
# would have been committed by the next person to clone.
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock
.claude/settings.local.json
.opencode/opencode-loop/

# The mutation harness's scratch state: what is mid-edit while a sweep runs,
# and the build output of cases that produced no test results, kept so a
# "COMPILE ERROR" verdict can be diagnosed rather than guessed at.
tools/.mutate-in-flight.json
tools/.mutate-failed-builds/

# Coverage and profiling output.
*.profraw
*.profdata
lcov.info
tarpaulin-report.*

# Editors and operating systems.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~
.idea/

# The dev tools are their own workspaces, so their build output is their own too.
tools/*/target/

# Agent skills fetched from somewhere else, and their manifest.
#
# Nothing matches these today and that is the point: `npx skills add` installs
# into the working directory unless it is given `-g`, and a checkout is the
# working directory an agent is usually standing in. Three arrived here that
# way — `clean-ddd-hexagonal`, `find-skills`, `rust-best-practices` — and they
# are the equivalent of `node_modules`: fetched, not authored here, and two of
# the three state no licence at all, so redistributing them is nobody's call.
# They live in `~/.agents/skills/` now, which is where they were meant to go.
#
# A skill written *for this repository* — how to add an agent adapter, how a
# migration goes — is the opposite case: it belongs in `.claude/skills/` and it
# belongs in the commit, the same way `AGENTS.md` does. So this names what gets
# fetched rather than the directory it lands in, because ignoring the directory
# would swallow the first one somebody writes without saying anything.
#
# Leteo's own skill is shipped and lives in `plugin/*/skills/memory/`.
#
# So there is no pattern here on purpose. A fetched skill and a written one land
# in the same place under the same shape, and nothing in the path tells them
# apart — which means any rule broad enough to catch the first swallows the
# second. Left visible instead: if `npx skills add` is ever run here without
# `-g`, `.claude/skills/`, `.agents/skills/` and `skills-lock.json` all turn up
# in `git status`, somebody sees it, and it is fixed with the flag. An ignore
# rule would have made the same mistake silent, which is how it went unnoticed
# for the five weeks it was already here.
